* Why my approach works: If a kid already has a lot of candies, or if adding extra candies makes them reach or beat the current maximum kid's score, they deserve a true. Otherwise, they get a false.

3. ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Brute Force with Nested Loops

* Idea: For every single kid, look at all the other kids to find the maximum count among them, then check if the current kid plus extra candies is greater than or equal to that maximum.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<bool> kidsWithCandies(vector<int>& candies, int extraCandies) {
        vector<bool> ans;
        for (int i = 0; i < candies.size(); i++) {
            int currentMax = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < candies.size(); j++) {
                if (candies[j] > currentMax) {
                    currentMax = candies[j];
                }
            }
            if (candies[i] + extraCandies >= currentMax) {
                ans.push_back(true);
            } else {
                ans.push_back(false);
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity: Time is O(n^2) because we loop through all kids for every single kid. Space is O(n) for the answer vector.
* What makes the next approach better: Finding the maximum score by scanning all kids every single time is a waste of effort. We can find the maximum just once before checking anyone.

Approach 2: Single Max Scan and Check (Your Approach)

* Idea: Find the maximum number of candies in the entire array once at the beginning. Then, walk through the array a single time, adding extra candies to each kid and comparing against that pre-calculated maximum.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<bool> kidsWithCandies(vector<int>& candies, int extraCandies) {
        int max_val = *max_element(candies.begin(), candies.end());
        vector<bool> ans;
        for (int i = 0; i < candies.size(); i++) {
            if ((candies[i] + extraCandies) >= max_val) {
                ans.push_back(true);
            } else {
                ans.push_back(false);
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity: Time is O(n) to find the max and check each kid. Space is O(n) for the answer vector.
* What makes the next approach better: While your approach is already optimal in time, we can make the C++ code cleaner by pre-sizing the result vector and avoiding repeated push_back overhead.

